    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new `deduplicate` method is placed sensibly \u2014 it sits alongside the other private static helpers (`customerCode`, `hash8`, `countNamesakes`), is named with a clear verb, is well-documented, and is wired in at the natural call site. The logic is in the right unit and nothing is a catch-all. One structural/idiomatic issue:\n\n- **Inline fully-qualified names break the file's import convention** (`BuildOwner.java:58`). The method uses `java.util.Set<String> existing = new java.util.HashSet<>()` inline, while every other type in this file is referenced by simple name with a top-of-file `import` (`StandardCharsets`, `MessageDigest`, `NoSuchAlgorithmException`, the officefloor/spring types). A maintainer of this file would add `import java.util.Set;` / `import java.util.HashSet;` and write `Set<String> existing = new HashSet<>();`. As written, the fully-qualified references read as foreign to the surrounding code.\n\nMinor observation (not blocking, no change required): `deduplicate` and `countNamesakes` each independently iterate `ownerRepository.findAll()`, so `service` now scans all owners twice. That's an efficiency/duplication point rather than a correctness one, and keeping the two concerns in separate, single-purpose helpers is the more readable structure \u2014 I'd leave them separate unless the scan cost becomes a concern.",
